## Overview
This guide explains how to build a frontend form in October CMS that allows users to submit data directly to the database. It uses the example of an "actor" form, building upon a previous contact form component but modifying it to save a model instead of sending an email.

## Creating the Actor Form Component
The recommended approach is to reuse existing code. The contact form component was copied from a general-purpose plugin and placed into the `movies` plugin, as actors are specific to that domain.

- **Source:** `plugins/watchlearn/contact/components/contactform/`
- **Destination:** `plugins/watchlearn/movies/components/actorform/`
- **Files Renamed:** `contactform.php` to `actorform.php`.

## Component PHP Logic (`ActorForm.php`)
The component's PHP class requires several modifications to function correctly.

1.  **Namespace:** Updated to `Watchlearn\Movies\Components`.
2.  **Class Name:** Renamed to `ActorForm`.
3.  **AJAX Handler:** The `onSend()` method was renamed to `onSave()` to better reflect its purpose.

### `onSave()` Method Implementation
This method contains the core logic for creating the database record.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> The logic follows the standard Laravel/Eloquent pattern: **instantiate**, **populate**, and **save**.

```php
// 1. Import the necessary classes
use Watchlearn\Movies\Models\Actor;
use Input;
use Flash;

// 2. Instantiate a new model
$actor = new Actor();

// 3. Populate model attributes from form input
$actor->name = Input::get('name');
$actor->last_name = Input::get('last_name');

// 4. Save the model to the database
$actor->save();

// 5. Provide user feedback
Flash::success('Actor added');
```

## Component Markup (`default.htm`)
The form's HTML is updated with relevant fields and, most importantly, the correct AJAX handler reference.

-   The form tag must include `data-request="onSave"` to connect the submit button to the `onSave()` method in the PHP class.
-   Input fields (`name`, `last_name`) are created to capture the actor's data.

## Adding User Feedback with Flash Messages
To inform the user of a successful submission, October's built-in `Flash` facade is used.

-   **In PHP:** After a successful save, `Flash::success('Actor added');` sets the message.
-   **In Twig (`default.htm`):** A special block is added to render the message on the page.

    ```twig
    {% flash success %}
        <div class="alert alert-success">
            {{ message }}
        </div>
    {% endflash %}
    ```

## Next Steps: Security
Currently, the form is public and can be used by anyone. The next step is to secure it so that only authenticated, logged-in users can add actors. This will be addressed by integrating the **RainLab.User plugin**.
